[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was filed as one issue with one fix. Its reports describe three
problems. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser is fixed and the booking-v3 form is
retired. The third problem, a one-day shift, is still open.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but the one-day shift is not fixed. Neither the parser fix nor retiring the form
moves a date by one day. Keep its ticket open until the cause is found.
</Callout>

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first.** Every tenant on file writes the day
first, so a booking for 7 April lands on 4 July. This is the bug #4471 names. It reaches people
through the app, email and the booking-v2 form. Fixed on 5 October:
`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form asks for the wrong order.** It prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date
field. People type what it asks for, month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day
first. Only booking-v3 tenants, and only through the form. The parser fix does not touch it.
Engineering will not fix it, because the booking-v3 form is retired.

**`shift`: dates move by one day.** No day and month swap moves a date by one day. These
reports say "Tuesday showed as Monday", and one client in Lisbon and one in Toronto saw different
days for the same meeting. That points at time zones, not date order. The desk noted these
reports (`desk-notes.json`) but counted them under `parser`.

## How they got merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports whose subject lines match.
People describe all three problems in the same words ("Date is wrong", "Wrong day on the
invite"), so the subject cannot tell them apart. Intake's tag cannot either: it guesses from the
reporter's browser, and the table counts how often that guess names another problem. What tells
them apart is the tenant (its locale and booking form), the channel, and what the report says
happened to the date.

## What to do

- Find where the one-day shift comes from. It is the only one of the three still open, and the
  last of its reports came in the day before #4471 closed.
- File new one-day reports under the shift ticket, not under `parser`, which is now fixed.
- Write a new decision record to replace 0001 for date reports: merge by cause, not by
  subject. Decision 0001 stays as it is; a new record replaces it.

